✅ Difference Between `new` and `newInstance()` in Java

Note: `new` is a **keyword/operator**. `newInstance()` is a **method** (reflection) — not an operator.

| Feature                | `new` Operator                              | `newInstance()` Method                                                              |
| ---------------------- | ------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Used for**           | Creating object when class is known         | Creating object **dynamically** at runtime                                          |
| **Syntax**             | `MyClass obj = new MyClass();`              | `MyClass.class.newInstance();` *(deprecated)* / better: constructor reflection      |
| **Type Checking**      | Compile-time                                | Runtime (can throw exceptions)                                                      |
| **Constructor Call**   | Any matching constructor you write          | `Class.newInstance()` needs **public no-arg** constructor                           |
| **Exception Handling** | `new` itself adds **no** checked exceptions (only if **your constructor** declares them) | Reflection API **always** declares checked exceptions (`InstantiationException`, `IllegalAccessException`, …) |
| **Performance**        | Faster                                      | Slower (reflection)                                                                 |
| **Status**             | Standard / preferred for normal code        | `Class.newInstance()` **deprecated since Java 9**                                   |


When class name is known at compile time → use `new`.
When class name comes from config / DB / CLI / plugin loader → use reflection (`newInstance` style).

────────────────────────────────────────
🔧 1. Using `new` (normal way)

```java
MyClass obj = new MyClass(); // compile-time, type-safe
```

- Simple and direct
- Compiler checks type/constructor
- Used most of the time

────────────────────────────────────────
🔧 2. Using `Class.newInstance()` (old reflection way)

```java
MyClass obj = MyClass.class.newInstance(); // ⚠️ deprecated since Java 9
```

Requires:
- A **public no-arg** constructor
- Exception handling (`InstantiationException`, `IllegalAccessException`)

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ 3. Modern preferred way (Java 9+)

```java
Class<?> clazz = Class.forName("com.example.MyClass"); // name from config/DB/file
MyClass obj = (MyClass) clazz.getDeclaredConstructor().newInstance();

// Or with args:
MyClass obj2 = (MyClass) clazz
        .getDeclaredConstructor(String.class, int.class)
        .newInstance("Afham", 5);
```

Why preferred over `Class.newInstance()`?
- Can call constructors **with parameters**
- Better exception handling (`InvocationTargetException` wraps real constructor errors)
- Not deprecated

────────────────────────────────────────
🧠 When is reflection-based creation useful?
- Class name unknown at compile time
- Plugin systems, factories driven by config
- Frameworks (Spring/Hibernate-style runtime wiring) — though frameworks usually hide this from you

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Summary (Interview Answer)
"`new` creates objects when the class is known at compile time (type-safe, fast).
`newInstance()` creates objects dynamically via reflection when the class name is resolved at runtime.
Prefer `Constructor.newInstance()` / `getDeclaredConstructor(...).newInstance(...)` today — `Class.newInstance()` is deprecated since Java 9."

────────────────────────────────────────
❗ Do both throw checked exceptions?
**No.**

- `new MyClass()` → no checked exception from `new` itself.
  Only if **your constructor** is declared as `throws SomeCheckedException` must you handle it.
- `Class.newInstance()` → **must** handle/declare `InstantiationException`, `IllegalAccessException`.
- `Constructor.newInstance(...)` → also checked: `InstantiationException`, `IllegalAccessException`, `InvocationTargetException`, etc.

```java
// new — usually no try/catch needed
MyClass a = new MyClass();

// Class.newInstance() — checked exceptions required
try {
    MyClass b = MyClass.class.newInstance();
} catch (InstantiationException | IllegalAccessException e) {
    e.printStackTrace();
}
```

🎤 Quick interview traps
1) `newInstance()` is a **method**, not an operator.
2) `Class.newInstance()` is **deprecated in Java 9** — mention the Constructor API.
3) `Class.newInstance()` only works with a **public no-arg** constructor.
4) Reflection creation is more flexible but slower and less type-safe.
5) Frameworks use reflection under the hood; in app code prefer `new` or DI.
6) Both do **not** always throw checked exceptions — only reflection APIs force InstantiationException/IllegalAccessException; `new` does not.
